WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.440
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:02.120 --> 00:00:05.687
A fringe pattern carries two pieces of information: where the bright bands

00:00:05.687 --> 00:00:05.880
sit,

00:00:05.880 --> 00:00:08.360
and how strongly brightness changes between bands.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:10.260
Let's separate those ideas.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:14.780
Use two coherent, narrow sources with the same frequency and polarization.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:20.240
On our distant screen, define positive y so that the phase difference we use

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:21.260
increases with y.

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:26.680
Write that phase as two pi times y divided by fringe width beta, plus a fixed

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:27.720
offset phi zero.

00:00:28.840 --> 00:00:32.730
Bright interference occurs when this total phase is an integer multiple of two

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:32.880
pi.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:36.800
A fixed offset moves the pattern but does not change the spacing.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:41.780
Take wavelength six hundred nanometres, screen distance one point five metres

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:45.280
and slit separation zero point nine millimetres.

00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:49.140
The small angle fringe width, wavelength times screen distance divided by slit

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:52.000
separation, is one millimetre.

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:54.900
Now let the fixed phase offset be positive pi over two.

00:00:55.540 --> 00:00:56.940
Where is the zero phase bright order?

00:00:58.040 --> 00:00:58.880
Pause and solve for y.

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:03.640
It is negative beta over four, or negative zero point two five millimetres in

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:05.000
our declared coordinate convention.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:10.300
Reversing the sign convention changes the signed description, so never quote a

00:01:10.300 --> 00:01:11.340
direction without defining it.

00:01:12.020 --> 00:01:15.473
Now make the two contributions have intensities nine and four units at the

00:01:15.473 --> 00:01:15.800
screen.

00:01:16.420 --> 00:01:19.640
These are the separate intensities each would give there, not their source

00:01:19.640 --> 00:01:20.040
powers.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:24.920
Superposition gives total intensity nine plus four plus twice the square root

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:26.760
of thirty six times cosine of the phase.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:29.780
At a bright order, the result is twenty five units.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:33.200
At an opposing phase, it is one unit, not zero.

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.440
Perfect coherence does not guarantee perfect darkness when amplitudes differ.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:42.000
Fringe visibility compares the maximum minus minimum to the maximum plus

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:42.300
minimum.

00:01:43.060 --> 00:01:47.139
Here it is twenty four divided by twenty six, or twelve thirteenths, about

00:01:47.139 --> 00:01:47.360
zero

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:49.380
point nine two three.

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:53.100
Equal intensities would give unity visibility in this ideal fully overlapping

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:53.420
model.

00:01:54.160 --> 00:01:58.260
Poor coherence, mismatched polarization and detector averaging can also reduce

00:01:58.260 --> 00:01:58.800
contrast.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:03.400
Our JEE routine is phase first for position, then the amplitude dependent

00:02:03.400 --> 00:02:04.780
intensity formula for brightness.

00:02:05.620 --> 00:02:08.860
Do not mistake a shifted fringe or a nonzero minimum for proof that

00:02:08.860 --> 00:02:09.800
interference has disappeared.

00:02:10.152 --> 00:02:13.872
Our earlier two slit model treated each slit as very narrow.

00:02:14.712 --> 00:02:17.812
Real slit width adds another layer: a diffraction envelope.

00:02:18.752 --> 00:02:22.272
Picture closely spaced interference ripples sitting inside a broader intensity

00:02:22.272 --> 00:02:22.732
profile.

00:02:23.952 --> 00:02:28.192
Assume equal, uniformly illuminated rectangular slits, coherent equal phase

00:02:28.192 --> 00:02:30.032
light, and far field conditions.

00:02:30.992 --> 00:02:34.432
Let each slit width be a and the centre to centre separation be d.

00:02:35.392 --> 00:02:39.212
Constructive interference orders obey d sine theta equals m wavelengths.

00:02:40.352 --> 00:02:45.032
Single slit envelope minima obey a sine theta equals p wavelengths, where p is

00:02:45.032 --> 00:02:45.992
a nonzero integer.

00:02:46.772 --> 00:02:50.672
If both conditions hold at the same direction, the interference order has no

00:02:50.672 --> 00:02:51.512
light to reinforce.

00:02:52.012 --> 00:02:52.572
It is missing.

00:02:53.612 --> 00:02:57.012
Dividing the two equations gives m equal to p times d over a.

00:02:57.812 --> 00:03:01.992
Try d equal to zero point six millimetres and a equal to zero point two

00:03:01.992 --> 00:03:03.032
millimetres.

00:03:03.032 --> 00:03:04.332
Their ratio is three.

00:03:05.112 --> 00:03:09.432
Every third nonzero interference order coincides with an envelope minimum in

00:03:09.432 --> 00:03:10.192
this ideal model.

00:03:10.932 --> 00:03:14.956
In particular, orders positive three and negative three are absent at the

00:03:14.956 --> 00:03:15.232
first

00:03:15.232 --> 00:03:15.972
envelope boundaries.

00:03:17.452 --> 00:03:21.352
Now use wavelength six hundred nanometres and screen distance one point five

00:03:21.352 --> 00:03:22.032
metres.

00:03:22.972 --> 00:03:25.692
Interference spacing is one point five millimetres.

00:03:25.692 --> 00:03:29.258
The first diffraction minima lie four point five millimetres from the centre

00:03:29.258 --> 00:03:29.352
on

00:03:29.352 --> 00:03:33.572
either side, so the full central envelope width is nine millimetres.

00:03:33.572 --> 00:03:36.712
How many principal interference orders fit strictly inside it?

00:03:37.652 --> 00:03:38.112
Take a moment.

00:03:38.892 --> 00:03:42.772
Their labels are negative two, negative one, zero, positive one and positive

00:03:42.772 --> 00:03:43.332
two.

00:03:43.892 --> 00:03:45.072
Five, not seven.

00:03:45.712 --> 00:03:47.952
The boundary orders are dark and must not be counted.

00:03:48.672 --> 00:03:50.612
The envelope also makes the outer peaks weaker.

00:03:51.292 --> 00:03:55.652
For a mathematical intensity sketch, multiply cosine squared of pi d y over

00:03:55.652 --> 00:04:00.932
wavelength D by the squared sine over argument envelope, using argument pi a y

00:04:00.932 --> 00:04:02.012
over wavelength D.

00:04:02.592 --> 00:04:06.552
At zero argument, that envelope equals one by its limit; don't let your

00:04:06.552 --> 00:04:08.092
calculator divide zero by zero.

00:04:08.712 --> 00:04:12.272
The envelope can shift actual local peak positions slightly away from the

00:04:12.272 --> 00:04:13.352
narrow slit order positions.

00:04:14.032 --> 00:04:17.352
Our five order count is the standard interference order description.

00:04:18.332 --> 00:04:21.892
Distinguish fringe spacing, full central envelope width, and actual peak

00:04:21.892 --> 00:04:22.968
brightness: they are different quantities in one pattern.

00:04:22.968 --> 00:04:25.308
Let's finish with a lovely optical puzzle.

00:04:26.248 --> 00:04:29.028
Two ideal crossed polarizers block the transmitted beam.

00:04:29.828 --> 00:04:33.048
Put another polarizer between them, and some light can come through.

00:04:33.688 --> 00:04:35.648
Does that mean the extra sheet creates energy?

00:04:36.228 --> 00:04:37.168
Absolutely not.

00:04:37.808 --> 00:04:41.588
Each sheet still reduces or preserves the intensity arriving at that sheet.

00:04:42.128 --> 00:04:46.088
It changes the selected polarization direction, which changes what the later

00:04:46.088 --> 00:04:46.848
sheet can transmit.

00:04:47.968 --> 00:04:50.728
Start with unpolarized light of intensity one hundred units.

00:04:51.148 --> 00:04:55.288
The first axis is zero degrees and the final axis is ninety degrees.

00:04:56.228 --> 00:04:58.688
The first ideal sheet passes fifty units.

00:04:59.588 --> 00:05:01.328
Insert one middle axis at angle theta.

00:05:02.028 --> 00:05:06.468
That sheet passes fifty times cosine squared theta, and the last passes that

00:05:06.468 --> 00:05:08.028
result times sine squared theta.

00:05:08.988 --> 00:05:12.788
Using the double angle relation, final intensity is one hundred divided by

00:05:12.788 --> 00:05:14.988
eight, times sine squared of twice theta.

00:05:15.668 --> 00:05:17.328
What angle gives the greatest output?

00:05:18.188 --> 00:05:18.888
Pause before choosing.

00:05:18.888 --> 00:05:23.788
Forty five degrees makes that squared sine one, so the maximum is twelve point

00:05:23.788 --> 00:05:24.608
five units.

00:05:25.328 --> 00:05:29.713
The final beam is polarized along the final sheet's ninety degree axis, not

00:05:29.713 --> 00:05:29.888
the

00:05:29.888 --> 00:05:30.848
middle sheet's axis.

00:05:31.788 --> 00:05:36.888
Now use two middle sheets instead, giving successive axes zero, thirty, sixty

00:05:36.888 --> 00:05:37.708
and ninety degrees.

00:05:38.408 --> 00:05:41.088
Each step after the first differs by thirty degrees.

00:05:41.748 --> 00:05:43.908
Cosine squared thirty degrees is three quarters.

00:05:44.728 --> 00:05:49.218
Track the sequence: fifty units, thirty seven point five, twenty eight point

00:05:49.218 --> 00:05:53.768
one two five, and finally twenty one point zero nine three seven five units.

00:05:54.548 --> 00:05:58.548
More than twelve point five, but every individual step still lost light.

00:05:59.168 --> 00:06:03.028
If you swap or reorder the sheets, their successive angle differences change

00:06:03.328 --> 00:06:04.428
and the result can change.

00:06:05.148 --> 00:06:08.948
If the original beam is already polarized, do not automatically halve its

00:06:08.948 --> 00:06:13.048
intensity at the first sheet.

00:06:13.048 --> 00:06:14.388
Use the angle between its actual polarization and that first axis.

00:06:15.088 --> 00:06:19.268
These are ideal monochromatic teaching models; real sheets have losses and

00:06:19.268 --> 00:06:22.568
leakage, so adding many sheets is not a promise of unlimited useful

00:06:22.568 --> 00:06:23.208
transmission.

00:06:23.968 --> 00:06:28.648
For JEE problems, draw the axes in order, update polarization after each

00:06:28.648 --> 00:06:31.408
filter, and multiply the correct intensity factors.

00:06:32.308 --> 00:06:36.039
Tracking the intermediate state is more reliable than applying one angle to

00:06:36.039 --> 00:06:36.188
the

00:06:36.188 --> 00:06:36.848
entire stack.
